# TASK 2 – COPY CONSTRUCTORS: SHALLOW AND DEEP COPIES

## 1. Introduction

A Prescription class stores medicine names in a dynamically allocated array. When an object is copied, a shallow copy copies only the pointer address. This causes both objects to share the same memory.

A deep copy creates a separate memory location for the copied object. This prevents unwanted changes and memory errors.

## 2. Shallow Copy Implementation

### C++ Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Prescription {
    int n;
    string* medicines;

public:
    Prescription(int count) {
        n = count;
        medicines = new string[n];
    }

    void set(int i, string m) {
        medicines[i] = m;
    }

    void display() {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            cout << medicines[i] << " ";
        cout << endl;
    }

    ~Prescription() {
        delete[] medicines;
    }
};

int main() {
    Prescription p(2);

    p.set(0, "Paracetamol");
    p.set(1, "ORS");

    Prescription q = p;  // Shallow copy

    q.set(1, "Cetirizine");

    cout << "Original: ";
    p.display();

    cout << "Copied: ";
    q.display();

    return 0;
}
```

### Observation

Both objects share the same memory address. Therefore, changing the copied prescription also changes the original prescription.

The program may crash at the end because both destructors attempt to delete the same memory.

### Memory Diagram – Shallow Copy

```text
       Object p                 Object q
    +------------+           +------------+
    | medicines  | --------> |            |
    +------------+           +------------+
                                  |
                                  |
                                  v
                         Shared Memory
                      +------------------+
                      | Paracetamol      |
