```

### Sanitizer Command

```bash
g++ -std=c++17 -fsanitize=address -g shallow.cpp -o shallow
./shallow
```

### Observation

The Address Sanitizer may report an attempt to free already freed memory (double-free).

## 3. Deep Copy Implementation

### C++ Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Prescription {
    int n;
    string* medicines;

public:
    // Parameterized constructor
    Prescription(int count) {
        n = count;
        medicines = new string[n];
    }

    // Copy constructor
    Prescription(const Prescription& p) {
        n = p.n;
        medicines = new string[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            medicines[i] = p.medicines[i];

        cout << "Copy constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // Copy assignment operator
    Prescription& operator=(const Prescription& p) {
        if (this != &p) {
            string* temp = new string[p.n];

            for (int i = 0; i < p.n; i++)
                temp[i] = p.medicines[i];

            delete[] medicines;

            medicines = temp;
            n = p.n;
        }

        return *this;
    }

    // Destructor
    ~Prescription() {
        delete[] medicines;
    }

    void set(int i, string m) {
        medicines[i] = m;
    }

    string get(int i) const {
        return medicines[i];
    }

    void display() const {
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            cout << medicines[i] << " ";
        cout << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Prescription p(2);

    p.set(0, "Paracetamol");
    p.set(1, "ORS");

    Prescription q = p;  // Copy constructor

    q.set(1, "Cetirizine");

    Prescription r(1);
    r = p;               // Copy assignment

    p.set(0, "Ibuprofen");

    cout << p.get(0) << " " << p.get(1) << " | ";
    cout << q.get(0) << " " << q.get(1) << " | ";
    cout << r.get(0) << " " << r.get(1) << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Expected Output

```text
Copy constructor called
Ibuprofen ORS | Paracetamol Cetirizine | Paracetamol ORS
```

### Explanation

- The copy constructor creates a new memory allocation and copies every medicine.
- The copy assignment operator copies the contents into a separate memory location.
- The original and copied objects can be modified independently.
- The destructor releases the allocated memory.

### Memory Diagram – Deep Copy

```text
       Object p                 Object q
    +------------+           +------------+
    | medicines  | --------> |            |
    +------------+           +------------+
                                  |
                                  v
                           Memory Block 1
                           Paracetamol
                           Cetirizine


       Object r
    +------------+
    | medicines  | --------> Memory Block 2
    +------------+           Paracetamol
```

Each object has its own separate memory allocation.

## 4. Design and Analysis

### 4.1 Why Does Shallow Copy Cause a Crash?

Shallow copying copies the pointer address instead of creating new memory. Both objects point to the same memory location.

When the objects are destroyed, both destructors try to release the same memory. This causes a double-delete error and may crash the program.

### 4.2 Rule of Three

The Rule of Three states that if a class requires one of the following functions, it usually requires all three:

1. Copy constructor
2. Copy assignment operator
3. Destructor

They are needed together because the class manages dynamically allocated memory.

### 4.3 Three Situations Where Copy Constructor Is Called

1. When one object is initialized using another object.
   Example: `Prescription q = p;`

2. When an object is passed to a function by value.

3. When an object is returned from a function by value, if copy elision does not remove the copy.

## 5. Experimental Investigation

### 5.1 Pass by Value and Pass by Const Reference

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Test {
public:
    Test() {}

    Test(const Test& t) {
        cout << "Copy constructor called" << endl;
    }
};

void passValue(Test t) {
    cout << "Passed by value" << endl;
}

void passReference(const Test& t) {
    cout << "Passed by const reference" << endl;
}

int main() {
    Test a;

    passValue(a);
    passReference(a);

    return 0;
}
```

**Expected Output**

```text
Copy constructor called
Passed by value
Passed by const reference
```

**Observation:**

- Pass by value creates a copy of the object.
- Pass by const reference does not create a copy.
- Therefore, const reference is more efficient for large objects.

### 5.2 Testing Self-Assignment

The following statement is used:

```cpp
p = p;
```

The condition `if (this != &p)` prevents unnecessary copying and protects the object during self-assignment.

### 5.3 Measuring Copying Time

The following code measures the time required to copy prescriptions containing different numbers of medicines.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <chrono>
using namespace std;
using namespace chrono;

class Prescription {
    int n;
    string* medicines;

public:
    Prescription(int count) {
        n = count;
        medicines = new string[n];
    }

    Prescription(const Prescription& p) {
        n = p.n;
        medicines = new string[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            medicines[i] = p.medicines[i];
    }

    ~Prescription() {
        delete[] medicines;
    }
};

int main() {
    int sizes[] = {10, 1000, 100000};

    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        Prescription p(sizes[i]);

        auto start = high_resolution_clock::now();

        for (int j = 0; j < 100; j++) {
            Prescription q = p;
        }

        auto stop = high_resolution_clock::now();

        auto time = duration_cast<microseconds>(stop - start);

        cout << "Medicines: " << sizes[i]
             << " Time: " << time.count()
             << " microseconds" << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

**Observation:**

The copying time increases as the number of medicines increases because every medicine must be copied into the new memory allocation.

