## 6. Conclusion

The compiler-generated copy constructor can be used when a class does not directly manage resources such as dynamically allocated memory.

When a class contains pointers and manages dynamic memory, a custom copy constructor is needed to perform a deep copy.

Deep copying ensures that objects remain independent and prevents memory errors such as double deletion.

## 7. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis

If medicine names are stored using `vector<string>` instead of a dynamically allocated array, the compiler-generated copy constructor automatically performs a deep copy.

### Alternative C++ Program Using Vector

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Prescription {
    vector<string> medicines;

public:
    Prescription(int n) {
        medicines.resize(n);
    }

    void set(int i, string m) {
        medicines[i] = m;
    }

    string get(int i) {
        return medicines[i];
    }
};

int main() {
    Prescription p(2);

    p.set(0, "Paracetamol");
    p.set(1, "ORS");

    Prescription q = p;

    q.set(1, "Cetirizine");

    cout << p.get(0) << " " << p.get(1) << endl;
    cout << q.get(0) << " " << q.get(1) << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Output

```text
Paracetamol ORS
Paracetamol Cetirizine
```

### Complexity Comparison

| Copy Type | Time Complexity | Space Complexity |
|---|---|---|
| Shallow Copy | O(1) | O(1) |
| Deep Copy | O(n) | O(n) |
| Vector Copy | O(n) | O(n) |

Here, n represents the number of medicines.

### Comparison

- Shallow copy only copies the pointer address, so it takes O(1) time and space, but it is unsafe.
- Deep copy creates a separate array and copies all medicines, taking O(n) time and O(n) additional space.
- Vector automatically manages memory and performs deep copying.
- Vector is safer and reduces the need to write a custom copy constructor, assignment operator and destructor.

### Final Conclusion

Using `vector<string>` is generally safer and easier because it automatically manages memory. However, implementing a deep-copying constructor manually helps understand dynamic memory allocation, pointers and the Rule of Three.
